cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Seamark in academic work, or reproduce its measurements, please
  cite it as below. The figures a paper is most likely to quote are in
  docs/FIGURES.md, which is generated by scripts/figures.py from the source
  tree itself: every figure is regenerated by `make figures` from the tree it
  describes, and `make release-check` refuses a tree whose figures and code
  disagree. Cite the version together with the commit, because a version is a
  claim about an interface and a commit is the thing that was measured.
title: "Seamark: change control for what AI coding agents can do"
abstract: >-
  Seamark reads the configuration files AI coding agents load, resolves what
  they add up to across the scopes each vendor merges, and reports what an agent
  can do in a repository or on a machine. It runs nothing: no agent binary, no
  hook, no referenced script, and no model on the decision path. Readers touch
  the disk; resolution and rules are pure functions over what was read.
  Three claims are made and no others. SURFACE is what an agent can do here,
  with every capability citing the file it came from, the vendor's documented
  merge rule together with the URL and version that states it, and the rule that
  names it. CHANGE is which capability appears, disappears, widens or narrows
  between two moments. CURRENCY is whether an approval still describes what is
  there, bound to the digest of what was approved rather than to its name or its
  date, so an approval expires by itself when the thing it covered changes.
  Four constraints are load-bearing. Nothing is ever scored: a test greps every
  finished document for score, grade, rating and percent and fails on any of
  them. Nothing is judged, only cited, so a finding names a rule whose norm was
  written by somebody else. Nothing unobserved is inferred: a capability that
  could not be resolved is INDETERMINATE, counted apart and never distributed
  across the answers that were. And nothing is acted on: an exit code informs,
  and whether that blocks anything is the user's own branch protection.
  The published limits are part of the claim rather than a caveat on it. The
  governing one is that configuration declares and does not demonstrate
  behaviour: a hook that is written is not a hook that ran, and a hook that is
  absent is not proof that nothing ran. Only what is on disk is visible, merge
  semantics depend on the agent version, and a referenced script can change
  after it is read, which is why everything binds to a digest.
  This release ships both halves. check resolves the surface and diff says what
  changed between two moments; scan reads the sessions an agent recorded about
  itself and watch records one from outside the agent through an MCP proxy, with
  every record declaring the level it was captured at and what that level may
  not claim. Underneath all of it, an offline verifier over an RFC 6962 Merkle
  tree with Ed25519 signatures, integrity and identity kept as separate answers.
  Built by one author with heavy use of an AI coding assistant. The product
  decisions, the invariants and every rejected alternative in docs/DESIGN.md are
  the author's; the assistant wrote a large share of the implementation and the
  tests against them. README.md states this in full, and the gates in the
  repository exist because assistance at that speed produces exactly the failure
  they catch.
